WEBVTT

00:00.151 --> 00:04.812
where I only pushed it once, and I thought it was recording, and I did a five-hour interview.

00:06.793 --> 00:07.773
None of it was recorded.

00:08.253 --> 00:09.633
Yeah.

00:09.833 --> 00:11.974
So today is May 8, 2026.

00:12.014 --> 00:14.954
My name is Caitlyn Sheets, and I am here with Dr. G. Cliff Lamb.

00:15.155 --> 00:21.536
We're conducting this interview in the Ag Life Science Building on the campus of Texas A&M University in College Station, Texas.

00:21.976 --> 00:28.038
The interview is part of an oral history project documenting the experiences of Aggies for the 150th celebration of Texas A&M.

00:28.318 --> 00:29.678
Do I have consent to record this?

00:30.238 --> 00:30.459
Yes.

00:30.739 --> 00:30.999
Awesome.

00:31.019 --> 00:31.380
Thank you.

00:32.321 --> 00:33.662
Thank you for sharing your story with me.

00:34.503 --> 00:40.891
To begin, can you give us a little brief bio kind of information about where you're born, where you grew up, that type of thing?

00:41.912 --> 00:42.292
Sure.

00:42.593 --> 00:42.853
Yeah.

00:42.893 --> 00:46.197
So, yeah, thanks a lot for taking the time to chat.

00:47.735 --> 00:50.177
So I have a very unique background.

00:50.577 --> 00:53.480
I actually grew up in Zimbabwe, Africa.

00:53.860 --> 00:54.400
Wow.

00:54.420 --> 01:00.926
So South Central Africa on a beef, cattle, dairy, corn and tobacco operation.

01:01.186 --> 01:02.067
How interesting.

01:02.147 --> 01:03.628
So throughout my whole career.

01:04.230 --> 01:05.770
I was very interested in agriculture.

01:05.790 --> 01:08.891
I knew at a very young age I was going to come to the U.S. and study.

01:09.851 --> 01:21.574
And so in the late 80s, I came and I studied at Middle Tennessee State University, got my bachelor's degree, and then went to Kansas State and got my master's and PhD.

01:22.994 --> 01:25.995
I've always been highly passionate in reproductive biology.

01:26.855 --> 01:51.363
and using reproduction to increase efficiency of livestock operations yeah so so that's uh that was my uh formal education and then from there i had full intentions to go back to africa at that point that was always my goal and in the late 1990s early 2000s there was a big uh push by the government of zimbabwe to nationalize

01:52.177 --> 02:20.987
all of the farms and ranches so basically um the operation i grew up on and many of the operations uh those operations were nationalized and so there was not a real large incentive for me to return to africa because didn't really have anything that we could sort of take over and and operate and so i took a position at the university of minnesota as a faculty member

02:21.970 --> 02:25.894
I spent 10 years as a faculty member at the University of Minnesota and loved it.

02:26.614 --> 02:27.735
Really enjoyed being there.

02:28.736 --> 02:39.946
Following that, I was asked to essentially do some work as an administrator and a faculty member at the University of Florida, and I was there for about 10 years.

02:39.966 --> 02:40.547
Wow.

02:42.819 --> 02:50.448
I'm in my 10th year now at Texas A&M.

02:50.468 --> 02:57.736
I came here as the department head in March of 2017 as department head of animal science.

02:59.078 --> 03:03.583
I spent five years as department head of animal science.

03:04.753 --> 03:11.960
and also interim department head for ecology and conservation biology and other departments on campus.

03:12.521 --> 03:22.732
At that time, that department was restructured with wildlife and fisheries into two separate departments, and I served as the interim department head for rangeland wildlife and fisheries management.

03:23.272 --> 03:25.556
at the same time as being department head of animal science.

03:26.076 --> 03:28.320
It's a lot of work.

03:28.760 --> 03:30.062
Yeah, it sure is.

03:30.103 --> 03:31.304
But a great experience.

03:31.725 --> 03:35.871
And then in December of 2021, the chancellor...

03:37.764 --> 03:45.712
of the Texas A&M University System had called me and asked me if I would be interested in being the director of AgriLife Research.

03:45.752 --> 03:55.022
And so I saw a lot of opportunity, a lot of opportunity to grow our agency and took that position.

03:55.082 --> 03:56.383
And I've been in this role since

03:57.584 --> 03:58.484
January of 2022.

03:59.445 --> 04:00.305
Fabulous.

04:00.845 --> 04:04.146
Many roles and much experience.

04:04.326 --> 04:14.050
For someone who is ignorant, going back a little bit on what you're talking about, what does it mean when they took the farms, the ranches and made it state?

04:15.086 --> 04:19.687
Yeah, so they nationalized about 6,000 farms and ranches.

04:19.947 --> 04:23.308
In other words, the government took them away from the landowners.

04:25.629 --> 04:29.910
Most people whose farms were taken, they had about 90 days to leave the land.

04:31.151 --> 04:33.652
And then they basically...

04:35.988 --> 04:57.846
uh redistributed the land to other people either people from the city or uh government officials received land wow how disruptive hugely disruptive uh and really what it did zimbabwe for people who don't know was had the reputation uh the name of being the bread bread basket of africa um so we were producing

04:59.067 --> 05:24.352
enough food to feed the whole country and we exported a ton of food to neighboring countries and within three years we were producing less than 20 percent of the food that we were producing three years before as a result of the nationalization and Zimbabwe's economy at the time was largely driven on agriculture and the economy imploded to the point that inflation was so bad

05:25.090 --> 05:29.732
That it was over 175% a day, inflation-wise.

05:30.832 --> 05:32.273
Oh, wow.

05:32.613 --> 05:33.774
It was a real disaster.

05:33.794 --> 05:35.794
So basically the country went bankrupt.

05:36.094 --> 05:36.795
Yeah.

05:37.135 --> 05:39.096
Thank you for the history.

05:39.136 --> 05:41.497
Listen, that is out.

05:41.597 --> 05:43.818
Out to the people who... Yeah, absolutely.

05:43.858 --> 05:44.038
Yeah.

05:48.056 --> 05:50.117
Sorry, just digesting that.

05:51.338 --> 05:56.963
So you said you were always interested in cattle and reproductive aspect of it.

05:57.003 --> 05:58.344
What kind of drew you to that?

05:59.325 --> 06:07.933
So growing up on a fairly large ranch and farm, we both were farmed and ranched.

06:08.873 --> 06:11.155
And so we had dairy cattle and we had beef cattle.

06:11.616 --> 06:12.977
And from a very young age,

06:13.971 --> 06:39.303
um i'm not sure what it was that drew me to specifically reproduction in cattle um i was highly passionate about um about livestock i mean from a very very young age as early as i can remember i just loved working on the farm but uh we had a neighbor that was one of the only people in the country who did

06:40.063 --> 06:56.560
embryo transfer and embryo transfer is basically taking embryos from genetically superior animals and take causing that cow to produce more than she normally does and then taking those embryos and transferring them

06:57.123 --> 07:03.848
to surrogates or recipients that are not as genetically good, but they give birth to a genetically superior animal.

07:05.490 --> 07:14.336
So I used to just, I loved, I mean, when he was doing it on our farm, it just sort of captured my attention.

07:15.718 --> 07:22.523
And when I was in elementary school, in fact, nobody in my family can remember me ever not talking about

07:23.763 --> 07:26.085
specializing in reproduction.

07:27.366 --> 07:30.729
My whole life, that's what I was just passionate about.

07:30.789 --> 07:38.336
And so throughout high school, I learned to artificially inseminate cows at a very young age.

07:38.496 --> 07:50.246
I was in high school, very early in high school, and very fortunate to have had the opportunity then to specialize in that through my career as a researcher.

07:52.155 --> 07:59.358
That's absolutely fascinating that it's not just that you're interested in cattle, but this is a very specific part of the cattle too.

07:59.418 --> 07:59.938
So, wow.

08:02.379 --> 08:07.461
What do you to specifically the academic and research side of things rather than the industry side of things?

08:10.183 --> 08:11.203
Really good question.

08:12.243 --> 08:20.707
To be perfectly honest, my goal after I got a PhD was to return and use the skills that I developed

08:21.598 --> 08:26.061
in growing agriculture back in Zimbabwe.

08:26.961 --> 08:38.608
And that was to not only improve production efficiency of cattle operations, beef cattle or dairy operations, but I also had a passion in wildlife.

08:38.688 --> 08:46.272
And so potentially utilizing the skills that I'd learned to then do some conservation work.

08:46.392 --> 08:50.635
So preserving genetics of endangered species and things like that.

08:53.101 --> 09:19.511
When I got word that I was not going to be able to go back and do anything on the farm or ranch, I sort of had a choice to either go into the U.S. industry on the commercial side, working with either a pharmaceutical company or a nutrition company or working in the industry somewhere or starting my own company.

09:21.112 --> 09:43.007
or to go into research and while i was working on my phd i became really intrigued it was a very interesting time of year for where for for where i had an interest and we were at that time developing a system to inseminate cows

09:44.029 --> 09:46.992
where we did not have to know when they were in heat.

09:47.232 --> 09:49.774
So we called it fixed-timed artificial insemination.

09:50.394 --> 09:57.661
So you take a group of cows, and typically you would have to make sure that the cow was receptive to become pregnant, right?

09:57.681 --> 09:58.541
She'd be in heat.

09:59.562 --> 10:00.843
And so if you had, say, 100 cows,

10:03.303 --> 10:08.848
If you wanted to get them all pregnant, you go out there and watch them and see when they come in heat, then you artificially inseminate them.

10:09.388 --> 10:19.698
We were in the process at that time of developing a system where you could inseminate them all at the same time on the same day, and it just makes things more efficient for a producer.

10:20.318 --> 10:40.723
So we started working on that while I was in my PhD, and I thought, you know, this is an opportunity to have a significant global impact in production systems for beef cattle where you can use reproduction to improve production efficiency of cattle operations.

10:41.323 --> 11:03.899
And so the first 10 years of my research career, that really was my primary focus, is refining that system, working on products that were not approved in this country at the time, working to get them approved so that cattle producers had access to those products, and then being able to refine it.

11:08.503 --> 11:15.447
excuse me, refine it in such a way that it was efficient for cattle producers to use.

11:15.727 --> 11:29.133
And, you know, you go through this process when you're working on your master's and PhD, that when you first start your master's, you really don't understand what research is, to when I finish my PhD,

11:31.455 --> 11:42.643
I was really intrigued by the scientific method, the process, understanding all of the things that have been done, and then coming up with ideas to make a difference in the future.

11:43.544 --> 11:48.948
By the time I finished my PhD, I could see myself in a role.

11:48.988 --> 11:50.289
You're knee-deep in it.

11:50.469 --> 11:50.909
Exactly.

11:51.950 --> 11:53.792
Is that system still in use today?

11:54.652 --> 11:55.193
Absolutely.

11:55.333 --> 12:00.397
So I'll give you some examples.

12:00.517 --> 12:15.949
When we started, so between 1997 and 2003 is sort of when we really developed and refined these systems, more than half of the cows that are artificially inseminated in the U.S. today are

12:16.973 --> 12:39.661
use the system we developed in 2003 and so that is happening today and we also in 2002 we started what's called the beef reproduction task force and so to the scientists who work in this space around the country a few of us got together to start the task force and

12:40.768 --> 12:50.654
we came up with a set of recommendations for beef producers to use on what systems to use to get their cows pregnant to artificial insemination.

12:51.555 --> 12:55.878
About half of the protocols that are on that sheet were ones that we played

12:56.507 --> 12:58.888
we developed or played a major role in developing.

12:58.968 --> 13:13.331
And so in the U.S., very, very fortunate that about 65 to 70 percent of all the heifers that are inseminated right now use a protocol that we had a hand in developing in some way.

13:14.112 --> 13:16.232
And then more than 50 percent of the cows.

13:16.852 --> 13:18.473
That's so impressive.

13:18.713 --> 13:19.753
Yeah.

13:20.838 --> 13:27.565
Globally, I think that's even is where we might have even had a greater role.

13:28.245 --> 13:37.394
And so you take a look and 2002, 2003 was the first time I visited Brazil.

13:38.234 --> 13:43.860
And Brazil obviously is the next largest beef producer in the world behind the US.

13:45.141 --> 13:50.826
And we started talking about using these systems to get cows pregnant in Brazil.

13:51.607 --> 13:58.513
At the time, less than a million cows in Brazil were artificially inseminated through fixed-timed artificial insemination.

13:59.254 --> 14:01.115
Today, about 18 million.

14:04.140 --> 14:22.507
every year are inseminated using a system or a variation of a system we developed in the late 90s and early 2000s so yeah yeah so huge impact there in australia about 75 percent of beef cattle producers that are inseminated use the system we developed

14:22.927 --> 14:25.269
So this is a global kind of impact.

14:25.670 --> 14:26.731
That is so impressive.

14:27.632 --> 14:35.100
What are some things that you've, like new things added to it that you've seen in the years?

14:35.954 --> 14:36.835
Yeah.

14:36.855 --> 14:47.946
So, you know, like I said, in those first 10 years, sort of 97 to 2007, we spent a lot of time refining these systems.

14:48.006 --> 14:53.072
And I've continued to work in that space to even refine things a little bit further.

14:53.752 --> 14:56.896
But we've had the advent of...

14:58.362 --> 15:06.785
the development of new technologies that then we incorporate into those types of systems.

15:06.805 --> 15:08.206
I'll give you one example.

15:09.206 --> 15:14.168
And very close to us here in Navasota is sexing technologies.

15:14.908 --> 15:20.430
It's a company that has a technology that can sex semen.

15:21.391 --> 15:33.923
And so you can inseminate a cow and the cow will give birth to over 90% accuracy that cow will give birth to a calf that is of a desired sex.

15:34.944 --> 15:42.632
So we took the systems we developed and developed fixed time AI systems where you could use sex semen.

15:43.152 --> 15:51.235
because most of the system we developed with conventional semen, and those two products are very different in the way that they're utilized.

15:51.255 --> 15:52.595
I didn't know that's possible.

15:53.516 --> 15:56.457
It's like science fiction, futuristic to me.

15:56.477 --> 15:57.097
It is, yes.

15:57.157 --> 15:57.777
Yeah, exactly.

15:58.398 --> 16:07.481
And then obviously I have big interests in other reproductive technologies, and so I've done a lot of work in in vitro fertilization,

16:08.241 --> 16:28.191
a little bit in cloning and it's an area that excites me right now and i have graduate students and postdoc students right now that are working on we call it precision genetics so it's using crisper cas9 which is a gene editing technology to try and create animals

16:29.454 --> 16:47.425
that are more productive I mean we have calves on the ground right now that have been edited for the slick gene and so just to explain how that works is that the slick gene is basically

16:48.305 --> 17:10.988
it's a prolactin receptor prolactin is used for milk is used for milk right it's a hormone that stimulates milk production but at the same time it's also tied to uh to animals that have about 10 of the hair of their normal of the normal kind and so we've edited those animals

17:11.889 --> 17:18.392
to have the slick gene so they have 10% of the hair of their full siblings.

17:18.432 --> 17:30.218
So full sibling, two brothers or two sisters can be standing side by side, and the one that's been edited for the slick gene has only 10% of the hair of the other one.

17:31.200 --> 17:32.501
And why is that important?

17:32.681 --> 17:35.562
Well, it's important because in Texas it's really hot.

17:37.103 --> 17:46.008
And so if they have less hair, they don't spend as much energy trying to cool down during the warm times of the year.

17:47.629 --> 17:51.472
That's so fascinating.

17:51.592 --> 18:02.600
When we take the temperature of these animals during the heat of the day, those animals that have this slick gene have a body temperature that's about a degree to a degree and a half less.

18:03.420 --> 18:25.286
in the middle of the day than those that have full hair that are full siblings so for warm climates you can ultimately breed these animals and they are more productive they survive better they get they're not as sick there's just a lot of these traits that will help them be more productive and so

18:26.886 --> 18:27.667
How creative.

18:29.488 --> 18:34.410
My first thoughts when I think of science and reproduction, I don't think creative, but that is so creative.

18:35.130 --> 18:35.670
That's right.

18:35.851 --> 18:36.731
Yeah, it is.

18:37.011 --> 18:45.335
So that's a simple gene to edit, and obviously that's where we are right now.

18:47.096 --> 18:53.379
You can do an edit to make animals not have horns.

18:54.099 --> 19:02.326
And so the reason that you would do that right now in the old days, cows needed horns to fight off predators.

19:02.506 --> 19:04.167
We really don't have to worry about that now.

19:04.228 --> 19:05.328
Right.

19:05.489 --> 19:14.416
But so what a lot of people do is they actually have to dehorn the animals manually, which becomes it's just not a nice procedure.

19:14.456 --> 19:14.796
Right.

19:15.016 --> 19:18.439
But if we breed them to not have horns, then you don't even have to do that.

19:18.479 --> 19:18.760
Yeah.

19:18.800 --> 19:19.020
Right.

19:20.230 --> 19:29.578
yeah but my ultimate goal is to try and create edits that are multi

19:31.612 --> 19:38.337
that sort of focuses on more than one gene and focus really on the disease side of things.

19:39.357 --> 19:52.146
And the reason for that is that I want cattle to, I want to be able to breed animals that are resistant to certain diseases or ailments that you don't have to treat.

19:52.706 --> 19:54.107
So you think of what happens then.

19:54.807 --> 19:58.068
They're more productive because they don't get sick.

20:00.149 --> 20:02.490
Fewer of them die, fewer of them get sick, and so on.

20:03.151 --> 20:08.093
But in addition to that is we have a significant reduction in the use of antibiotics.

20:09.273 --> 20:14.475
And, you know, there's a lot of discussion about antibiotic resistance, right?

20:14.515 --> 20:15.115
Right, right.

20:15.296 --> 20:19.877
And so how do we limit the use of antibiotics in animal agriculture?

20:19.897 --> 20:22.558
And this is one way that I see as a potential solution.

20:22.598 --> 20:23.619
That's so smart.

20:24.999 --> 20:29.541
As you said, two solutions right there, or two problems, one solution.

20:29.641 --> 20:30.982
One solution, yeah, exactly.

20:32.168 --> 20:37.011
Is there any things that you can share that your protégés are working on?

20:37.111 --> 20:39.352
Anything you're... Yeah.

20:39.592 --> 20:44.455
I mean, so right now we have graduate students that are working in

20:45.515 --> 21:11.342
anywhere from the really applied side of things and when i say applied it's things that are immediately applicable to a producer right so our results might have application to them this year or next year right and then we have some very fundamental things that you know results might only help us in 10 or 15 years from now so i'll give you an example

21:14.137 --> 21:34.426
of something that we're working on in the short term and that is utilizing different products to give to cows around the start of the breeding season so we're not even worrying about whether they're artificially inseminated because still there are so many cows out

21:35.286 --> 21:50.315
that you can't handle very easily but if you could just have them once what can you do to treat them that they become pregnant sooner in the breeding season and they give birth to their calves sooner in the calving season

21:51.208 --> 22:12.774
and those calves end up becoming a lot more productive than if they don't become pregnant sooner in the breeding season so we're working on a couple of projects in that space then sort of in the intermediate space we have a graduate student right now working on utilizing doing in vitro fertilization

22:16.193 --> 22:30.067
and we're using seminal plasma so when a bull ejaculates semen right there's a lot of it's not just the sperm but there's a lot of fluids around the sperm that has has a lot of components

22:31.148 --> 22:40.558
Well, we have data from multiple bulls that are very high fertility, and we have bulls that are low fertility, right?

22:40.578 --> 22:41.699
And we have all of this data.

22:41.759 --> 22:49.507
And so what we've done is we're collecting seminal plasma from the two groups and then going to see if we can fertilize embryos

22:50.107 --> 22:57.535
and understand what it is about the seminal plasma in those two groups that causes differences in fertility.

22:57.915 --> 22:58.135
Wow.

22:58.276 --> 22:58.896
Does that make sense?

22:58.916 --> 22:59.437
Yeah, it does.

22:59.517 --> 22:59.957
Absolutely.

23:00.578 --> 23:06.764
And then obviously the thing that I talked about is on the gene editing front.

23:07.225 --> 23:11.069
And there are several things that we're working on.

23:13.751 --> 23:14.512
That's very exciting.

23:14.532 --> 23:17.174
A layperson's question.

23:18.676 --> 23:30.106
So the insemination, when you inseminate all of them at once instead of having to wait, does that create a situation where all of them are pregnant, thus give birth at the same time too?

23:30.726 --> 23:31.607
That's a great question.

23:31.767 --> 23:32.488
Yeah.

23:32.988 --> 23:36.231
So gestation of a cow is about 280 to 285 days.

23:36.251 --> 23:36.391
Okay.

23:38.745 --> 24:06.513
so about 10 days longer than a human and so yes they're all pregnant let's say on the same day um and when they go to calve they're usually spread out about at the max about three weeks okay so not unlike humans right so humans give birth 10 days early and some humans give birth 10 days later so that would be very similar in cattle

24:07.291 --> 24:16.935
When we do mass inseminate, you get somewhere around 50 to 60 percent of them pregnant.

24:17.035 --> 24:27.059
So not all of them will get pregnant, you know, and cattle are highly fertile because they've been selected for being fertile, because if they don't produce a calf,

24:28.017 --> 24:31.843
They consume resources, so they're eliminated, right?

24:32.143 --> 24:32.323
Yeah.

24:32.544 --> 24:43.179
So that percentage is very high, 50 to 60 percent for a single offspring bearing species, like a cow that generally only gives birth to one.

24:45.074 --> 24:47.575
They usually only give birth to one animal at a time.

24:48.156 --> 24:50.156
So litters are very different, right?

24:50.176 --> 24:53.177
You can get a pig pregnant almost any time you're inseminator.

24:53.638 --> 24:58.019
You've just got to hope you get more piglets than one or two.

24:58.639 --> 25:00.140
But cows only give birth to one.

25:01.981 --> 25:05.662
In comparison, and I give this, I tell people this all the time.

25:05.722 --> 25:13.145
So 50 to 60% of those cows get pregnant, but humans are about half that.

25:14.761 --> 25:15.642
Fertility.

25:15.742 --> 25:19.386
Humans are the least fertile species that give birth to one animal.

25:19.406 --> 25:19.866
Really?

25:19.926 --> 25:20.166
Yes.

25:21.067 --> 25:24.451
Because we don't select humans for fertility.

25:25.311 --> 25:25.552
True.

25:25.612 --> 25:30.016
And, you know, even in the wild, there's selection for fertility, right?

25:30.516 --> 25:31.117
Right.

25:31.717 --> 25:37.083
But humans, we try to figure out ways that every woman who wants to get pregnant...

25:37.823 --> 25:40.924
We try and get them pregnant in some way.

25:40.944 --> 25:42.365
Right, right.

25:42.645 --> 25:45.586
And so we're a very infertile species.

25:46.587 --> 25:48.247
That really puts it in perspective too.

25:48.447 --> 25:49.208
Yeah.

25:49.448 --> 25:55.911
And think whatever, think everything that they don't give birth all at the same time because that would be a disaster.

25:56.291 --> 25:56.791
It would.

25:56.951 --> 25:57.551
It would truly.

25:58.492 --> 26:05.455
But it is a lot of people don't realize that only about one in three times that

26:07.532 --> 26:15.539
they ovulate, do they act and or obviously through fertilization and things like that.

26:15.679 --> 26:17.141
Only about one in three survive.

26:17.541 --> 26:17.881
Wow.

26:18.222 --> 26:18.662
In humans.

26:19.163 --> 26:20.183
I did not know that either.

26:20.944 --> 26:21.665
People know that.

26:21.685 --> 26:22.165
Yes.

26:22.626 --> 26:30.673
Because a lot of humans don't know that they're pregnant or there's a fertilized egg and the egg dies before they

26:34.540 --> 26:37.162
That makes sense.

26:38.302 --> 26:40.904
Thank you for going to the little nitty-gritties of that.

26:42.705 --> 26:46.467
So, you were in Florida before Texas A&M, you said.

26:47.648 --> 26:52.691
How did the opportunity arrive to you for this original position here?

26:53.272 --> 26:53.892
At Texas A&M?

26:53.992 --> 26:54.232
