34:29.862 --> 34:35.687
So what were some key questions that you set out to answer throughout your work?

34:36.248 --> 34:41.412
I mean, we've gone over some of them, but even now for questions for the future.

34:41.432 --> 34:41.532
Yeah.

34:43.110 --> 34:45.891
Yeah, so I can share this in a couple of...

34:46.831 --> 34:58.435
So from my research background, some key questions, which we've talked about, is how do you use reproduction to improve production efficiency?

35:00.176 --> 35:08.119
When we hear people talking about sustainability of agricultural systems today, everybody talks about...

35:09.225 --> 35:12.667
methane and all of these things, right?

35:13.087 --> 35:15.968
And how do you reduce methane in animals?

35:17.589 --> 35:23.112
And there's talks on climate change and the impacts on water and all of those things.

35:23.874 --> 35:31.985
But the number one factor that affects sustainability of livestock operations is getting them pregnant.

35:33.046 --> 35:39.956
And if they're not pregnant, it doesn't matter how much less they consume to produce more or how much less methane or whatever.

35:42.158 --> 35:43.360
They're inefficient, right?

35:43.580 --> 35:45.842
And they're not sustainable.

35:46.763 --> 35:53.550
So using reproduction to increase sustainability of livestock production systems, and we still work on that today.

35:54.232 --> 36:05.518
That's an interesting idea from somebody from the outside that I didn't even think about that the more efficient the production is, reproduction is, the more sustainable it is.

36:05.758 --> 36:06.698
Absolutely.

36:07.499 --> 36:09.440
The common person doesn't put those two things together.

36:10.160 --> 36:10.621
Exactly.

36:10.661 --> 36:12.341
Yeah.

36:12.782 --> 36:18.725
And I feel bad that for some reason people have taken –

36:20.057 --> 36:41.505
cows or ruminants it's not just cows but certainly cows are the big thing but sheep and goats um and said you know they're producing greenhouse gases and getting and really it's such a small part of the total greenhouse gas emissions that even if we got rid of every cow we wouldn't change greenhouse gas emissions by

36:42.125 --> 36:50.022
more than half a percent right yeah and so if you get rid of every cow it's not going to change much yeah unfortunately we're

36:51.798 --> 37:16.457
uh the general population doesn't understand that because 98 98 of the population does not work directly in agriculture in this country correct and we have a social media i mean everybody makes it makes up their mind on very small snippets of things that they get to believe yeah so it's really unfortunate it's no context behind it yeah that makes sense

37:17.243 --> 37:17.443
Yeah.

37:17.743 --> 37:23.227
So, um, you know, questions that I ask now in my role as director of AgriLife Research.

37:23.267 --> 37:30.852
So AgriLife Research is, uh, it's a state agency, uh, within the Texas A&M university system.

37:31.532 --> 37:34.894
