We walk around with watches that keep track of

56:08.743 --> 56:14.588
heart rate and all of this stuff, you've seen these aura rings that people wear, right?

56:14.608 --> 56:20.834
That are always monitoring something about them, whether it's blood pressure, heart rates, things like that.

56:23.243 --> 56:50.997
um we're starting to use this technology right and it goes back to trying to identify animals that might be sick before they even act sick so yeah traditionally how we would do that is somebody's looking at the animals every day says oh that animal's got droopy ears it looks like it's not healthy we need to go and get its temperature and treat it well we're using this technology now to try and identify animals

56:52.012 --> 57:05.381
that are not doing well before they show clinical signs you can treat them sooner they recover quicker you use less antibiotics you use less treatments and

57:06.586 --> 57:10.809
That way you use, have less production losses.

57:10.989 --> 57:11.310
Right.

57:11.350 --> 57:11.990
Does that make sense?

57:12.010 --> 57:12.771
That absolutely does.

57:13.131 --> 57:21.998
And I know, I mean, I have a Garmin watch, and I'm out there all the time, and I can just tell by...

57:22.912 --> 57:36.593
how my uh the app is telling me it's like oh yeah you didn't sleep that great last night or you may be getting sick because of some some markers right and we're doing that with livestock

57:37.350 --> 57:42.152
I can, all I can picture is the cows wearing little smart watches.

57:42.612 --> 57:50.935
Yeah, so they wear ankle bracelets, like, or some have collars, some have ear tags.

57:50.995 --> 57:57.177
So there's various wearables that we have now for livestock.

57:57.757 --> 58:03.299
There's even a situation where they were working, have you seen these little dog robots?

58:03.359 --> 58:03.599
Mm-hmm.

58:04.521 --> 58:14.506
So one of the big issues in the fed cattle industry right now is some confined animals that are in feedlots just before they go to harvest.

58:16.627 --> 58:18.188
They have what are called pen riders.

58:18.248 --> 58:24.791
And these are people that will ride their horses through these pens every day to check the animals to make sure that they're healthy.

58:25.692 --> 58:31.815
Well, there's a company right now that is using a dog as a robot

58:32.747 --> 58:42.513
that is trained to look for animals that might not be well so that it can replace some of the pen writers because there's just not as many of them out there.

58:42.653 --> 58:43.173
Right.

58:44.334 --> 58:44.975
That is so neat.

58:45.135 --> 58:45.955
The future is now.

58:46.115 --> 58:47.316
The future is now, exactly.

58:47.356 --> 58:48.337
It's so interesting.

58:50.798 --> 58:56.782
Is there anything in your work in particular that you're very proud of and that'll be your shining mark?

58:59.855 --> 59:06.217
You know, I mean, I think that I'm the most proud of is the students that I've trained, right?

59:06.237 --> 59:09.839
I mean, the success that they had, right?

59:12.019 --> 59:15.681
There are some accomplishments that I think will...

59:17.113 --> 59:38.731
uh have long lasting impacts after i'm gone but yeah so certainly i'll say by the students that i've trained especially the graduate students i mean not that i i don't want to minimize the impact on undergraduate students but i i've been very fortunate to have had some phenomenal students that have

59:39.842 --> 59:42.964
If it wasn't for them, I would not have had the career I had.

59:42.984 --> 59:46.447
They're the ones that probably deserve as much credit.

59:46.947 --> 59:51.551
You know, where they've come up with great ideas and I've just figured out a way to facilitate it.

59:51.791 --> 59:52.111
Right.

59:52.832 --> 59:56.635
And so, you know, I've got...

01:00:00.277 --> 01:00:13.385
I think I have 10 students that are now faculty members and researchers at universities around the world, anywhere from the U.S. down to South America and Brazil, sorry, and Australia.

01:00:14.406 --> 01:00:17.327
And so, yeah, very proud of what they're doing.

01:00:18.898 --> 01:00:20.939
how they're training the next generation, right?

01:00:20.979 --> 01:00:24.840
And so some of them now already have students that are faculty members.

01:00:25.081 --> 01:00:25.901
Wow.

01:00:26.641 --> 01:00:29.202
And so to me, that's the proudest achievement.

01:00:31.443 --> 01:00:45.669
Obviously, seeing livestock producers around the world, especially when I travel and they say, you know, we use this system or we use this device that you helped develop.

01:00:46.429 --> 01:00:46.830
And it's...

01:00:48.017 --> 01:00:50.321
it helped us become more productive.

01:00:50.822 --> 01:00:53.567
Those are the things I really get a lot of joy out of.

01:00:54.950 --> 01:01:00.660
Um, so from my, in my current role, I think,

01:01:02.336 --> 01:01:26.016
the things that are going to be have impact for years to come are some of the infrastructure that i have been able to get built so and i'll give you some examples we just had a ribbon cutting on may 6 of last year so a year ago to open the new reproduction and biotecnology center out at the relis campus

01:01:27.475 --> 01:01:37.087
um and so that's it's the only facility of its kind in the world um we have just we had the groundbreaking last week for the new meat sites

01:01:39.435 --> 01:01:43.996
So that is something that I've worked on for eight years to get to this point.

01:01:45.617 --> 01:01:46.737
It has to feel so good seeing it.

01:01:46.757 --> 01:01:46.977
Yeah.

01:01:47.217 --> 01:02:01.981
And so to be able to figure out how to come up with $130 million and put all of that together and spend eight years getting to this point, those are things that I think will have impacts for the future.

01:02:02.922 --> 01:02:11.168
We did a ribbon cutting for a facility up in Canyon, in the High Plains.

01:02:11.268 --> 01:02:14.630
So there are researchers that are going to help

01:02:15.605 --> 01:02:32.028
whether it's cattle producers or wheat producers or whatever it is in the High Plains, my hope is that 30 years from now, as a result of having that facility, they generate results that have impacts for the agricultural folks in that region.

01:02:32.108 --> 01:02:33.408
Absolutely.

01:02:33.628 --> 01:02:35.089
It's absolutely global.

01:02:35.849 --> 01:02:41.970
That's what I'm figuring out from you is that one small little thing here can absolutely change the world.

01:02:41.990 --> 01:02:42.230
Yeah.

01:02:42.915 --> 01:02:43.716
Yeah, exactly.

01:02:43.836 --> 01:02:44.096
Wow.

01:02:44.396 --> 01:02:47.779
So yeah, so those are the things that I think.

01:02:47.819 --> 01:02:49.660
Yeah, absolutely.

01:02:50.441 --> 01:02:58.828
Specifically when you became the leader here at the department head, what were some of the goals or visions that you really wanted to bring in with you?

01:03:00.329 --> 01:03:08.155
Yeah, so let me talk not so much as department head, but as director of AgroLife Research, right?

01:03:11.668 --> 01:03:20.443
I wanted to modernize the agency and modernize internal controls so things that we did internally

01:03:23.317 --> 01:03:47.948
that we are using the latest technology so using electronic things using artificial intelligence to make decisions using data to make decisions and we've we've been able to modernize over time my goal was to increase research expenditures

01:03:49.147 --> 01:03:50.930
And that's important.

01:03:50.970 --> 01:04:00.447
The number itself isn't important, but if we are increasing research expenditures, it means that people who fund the work that we do,

01:04:02.307 --> 01:04:19.946
are seeing the importance of the work so that they fund it does that make sense absolutely and so we've been able to increase research expenditures by 53 in four years so that's unheard of 2022 when i started our research expenditures were 225 million this past year they were 345 million

01:04:24.672 --> 01:04:28.977
And so it's going after bigger, more complex problems.

01:04:30.018 --> 01:04:38.969
If you think back 60, 70, 80 years ago, most of the problems that we had to solve could be solved by individual little things, right?

01:04:39.129 --> 01:04:39.770
Right.

01:04:42.174 --> 01:04:45.777
I don't know, make a cow herd more productive.

01:04:46.317 --> 01:04:53.943
You could do one or two things that we did research on and that would make a significant impact on a cattle operation.

01:04:54.443 --> 01:04:56.265
Well, now it's more complex, right?

01:04:56.345 --> 01:05:00.088
And so how do we bring disciplines together?

01:05:00.108 --> 01:05:00.748
Right.

01:05:02.517 --> 01:05:11.362
So if we're trying to solve a cattle problem, it's not just people in the Department of Animal Science, right?

01:05:11.542 --> 01:05:15.104
You've got 70% of the cost of a cattle operation of feed.

01:05:16.005 --> 01:05:23.749
Feed is going to either be pastures or it's going to be... And so the people who grow that feed and how do you get them involved?

01:05:24.231 --> 01:05:27.454
How do you get the health people, the veterinarians involved?

01:05:28.254 --> 01:05:32.257
So bringing together, trying to solve problems in a more complex way.

01:05:32.538 --> 01:05:33.058
Absolutely.

01:05:34.679 --> 01:05:41.685
Does this mean that with every new innovation that the research side of it comes more nuanced?

01:05:43.253 --> 01:05:45.415
Is there a correlation there?

01:05:45.675 --> 01:05:45.915
Yes.

01:05:45.955 --> 01:05:50.559
So you mean the research is more nuanced?

01:05:50.579 --> 01:05:50.899
Yes.

01:05:50.919 --> 01:05:56.704
It is because some of the changes we make are so incremental, they don't make a big difference.

01:05:57.064 --> 01:05:58.886
But if you want to make big changes,

01:06:00.002 --> 01:06:21.130
it has to be a multidisciplinary approach that that has a look at holistically the whole enterprise right yeah um and whether it's i mean it could be water quality that has an impact on on ultimate production of chickens right and but if you're a chicken geneticist

01:06:22.148 --> 01:06:40.449
last thing you're thinking about is water right right and it doesn't matter how different the genetics are if the water is not good enough quality right and so how do you bring all those folks together to make big differences that makes sense make make people actually interact with each other yeah so

01:06:43.033 --> 01:06:44.634
So, yeah.

01:06:45.955 --> 01:06:59.926
The other thing, though, that I think has been really important for me is to focus on improving infrastructure across the state for research infrastructure, which allows people to become more productive.

01:07:00.727 --> 01:07:11.335
And when you think of all of our research infrastructure and agriculture across the state, most of that infrastructure was built 50 to 90 years ago.

01:07:11.415 --> 01:07:11.595
Wow.

01:07:13.424 --> 01:07:32.625
science is very different now technology is very different now so how do we get improved infrastructure with improved analytical techniques right you know it could be lab instrumentation whatever it is drones get that in the hands of people

01:07:33.280 --> 01:07:34.962
that then can go and do good work.

01:07:35.763 --> 01:07:37.064
And we think that's paying off.

01:07:37.584 --> 01:07:38.125
Absolutely.

01:07:38.565 --> 01:07:40.768
It sounds like it.

01:07:42.109 --> 01:07:52.800
Thinking back through your whole career, what have been some of the biggest challenges that you faced or that your team has faced when you're on teams?

01:07:53.941 --> 01:07:54.241
Yeah.

01:07:54.722 --> 01:07:55.883
Boy, that's...

01:08:01.256 --> 01:08:04.622
That's a really good question.

01:08:06.405 --> 01:08:12.856
I think challenges that we have had to overcome

01:08:14.461 --> 01:08:38.351
are artificial barriers that people put in front of you right so whether it's a bureaucratic process or a regulatory process or something along those lines that is a barrier for something good to happen right that makes sense and so trying to figure out

01:08:39.271 --> 01:09:07.388
creative ways to either get around it or to have conversations with the people that are making those decisions of why their decisions have an impact i would say that's been the biggest barrier um you know i think uh very often too it's the most researchers rely on funding to do the work

01:09:08.901 --> 01:09:31.462
and i've been very very fortunate that i've tended to always have a lot of funding but it's sometimes the best work isn't done with the funding you get done right and in other words some of my best research are things that we know is going to make a difference

01:09:32.673 --> 01:09:37.117
But you end up having to focus on something because that's what it's being funded.

01:09:37.598 --> 01:09:38.318
Does that make sense?

01:09:38.398 --> 01:09:39.359
Yeah, the grants.

01:09:39.419 --> 01:09:40.240
Yeah, exactly.

01:09:40.260 --> 01:09:42.782
And the competitive grant side of things.

01:09:43.463 --> 01:09:53.392
You spend so much time trying to focus on funding because that's a metric that you use to run your lab.

01:09:54.113 --> 01:09:57.556
But the impact for work very often isn't the work being funded.

01:09:57.716 --> 01:09:58.136
Right.

01:10:04.158 --> 01:10:06.420
I feel the pain over in the art side of things.

01:10:06.540 --> 01:10:08.442
Oh, absolutely.

01:10:09.142 --> 01:10:10.143
That's right.

01:10:10.764 --> 01:10:19.351
And obviously there's a lot more money in the STEM fields like ours.

01:10:20.072 --> 01:10:25.476
Over the course of your career, I know we've kind of touched on this, but over the course of your career, what has teaching meant to you personally?

01:10:26.457 --> 01:10:27.718
Teaching, yeah.

01:10:29.219 --> 01:10:33.805
I would, you know, for me, I probably lean on this more and say mentoring.

01:10:34.006 --> 01:10:34.446
Mentoring.

01:10:34.526 --> 01:10:35.407
Right.

01:10:36.309 --> 01:10:38.712
You know, I think...

01:10:41.592 --> 01:10:56.315
And I let a lot of my students know all the time, and even my own kids, that successful people don't become successful without other people believing in them.

01:10:56.715 --> 01:11:09.157
There's not a single person that can say they ended up doing what they were doing without looking back and going, boy, if this person didn't believe in me, I wouldn't have got this job or I wouldn't have got this position.

01:11:10.963 --> 01:11:36.246
and it's accepting the fact and being humble enough to accept the fact that you are going to have people believe in you or help you along the way and being comfortable with that I've seen so many bright people over the years that I don't know if it's arrogance I don't know if it is

01:11:38.505 --> 01:11:42.606
a lack of self-confidence, whatever it is, where they're not accepting help.

01:11:44.327 --> 01:11:53.990
And then 30 years later, they look back and go, geez, I didn't get done what I could have got done because I didn't listen.

01:11:56.031 --> 01:12:06.935
I know that is not quite answering the question you asked, but I think, yeah, I think mentoring to me means that

01:12:10.184 --> 01:12:22.229
you try and allow people to build on their strengths and understand what their weaknesses are and give them the opportunity to fly.

01:12:23.629 --> 01:12:34.454
And so if I focus on graduate students, right, every one of my students has a different skill set or has had a different skill set over the 30 years that I've been a faculty member.

01:12:36.945 --> 01:12:43.807
none of them are good at everything, but all of them have some phenomenal strengths.

01:12:44.307 --> 01:12:52.648
I can't say I've had a bad graduate student, like a student that I just was like, this is just going to be a disaster, right?

01:12:52.668 --> 01:12:53.109
Right.

01:12:53.929 --> 01:13:03.771
But it's, so being able to, as a mentor, being able to understand what their strengths are, help them on their weaknesses, and then

01:13:05.313 --> 01:13:13.159
provide some mentoring to let them succeed where I think that their best opportunity is.

01:13:13.379 --> 01:13:13.959
Does that make sense?

01:13:14.019 --> 01:13:14.820
Absolutely, it does.

01:13:15.800 --> 01:13:19.983
It honestly sounds like you were built, you were made to be a mentor.

01:13:20.604 --> 01:13:23.586
That's the energy I'm getting from you.

01:13:25.547 --> 01:13:34.434
Yeah, and I will admit, right, I don't personally, I don't get a lot of

01:13:36.035 --> 01:14:00.313
energy or pleasure i enjoy speaking to classes but teaching a class every day some people are just really really good at that that's not me i i love speaking to students anywhere from kindergarten age all the way up

01:14:01.672 --> 01:14:18.723
but I don't get a lot of energy, um, teaching a class to career undergraduates or even graduate students where, um, it's a routine every Monday, Wednesday, Friday.

01:14:19.163 --> 01:14:20.804
That just, it doesn't excite me.

01:14:20.925 --> 01:14:22.686
I just don't get a lot of energy out of it.

01:14:22.766 --> 01:14:24.247
I mean, I far prefer to do

01:14:25.125 --> 01:14:31.930
a lot more one-on-one mentoring or mentoring a group that's sort of working in a mission.

01:14:32.090 --> 01:14:32.351
Right.

01:14:32.811 --> 01:14:41.558
And I really take my hat off to those teachers that do a phenomenal job because they're so good at it.

01:14:41.918 --> 01:14:43.079
And that's just not my strength.

01:14:43.979 --> 01:14:44.880
That makes sense.

01:14:44.940 --> 01:14:45.140
Yeah.

01:14:45.821 --> 01:14:48.023
As you said, we all can't be good at everything.

01:14:48.063 --> 01:14:48.343
Yeah.

01:14:50.602 --> 01:14:57.316
Thinking back to your education, is there any professors or mentors that stick out in your mind as

01:14:59.603 --> 01:15:01.244
Like, those are the ones.

01:15:01.924 --> 01:15:07.025
Yeah, actually, yeah, there is almost every step of the way, right?

01:15:07.246 --> 01:15:20.590
I can name one or two people that were, so as an undergraduate student, I have a person whom I still remain in touch with today, who sort of took me under...

01:15:21.548 --> 01:15:24.349
his wing and really pushed me, right?

01:15:24.429 --> 01:15:42.476
I mean, he didn't just, um, I mean, I was a foreign student just new to this country, didn't know anybody, uh, but really, really pushed me and was somebody who helped me try and figure out where to go next.

01:15:44.459 --> 01:15:50.401
My mentor for my Master and PhD was a phenomenal scientist.

01:15:51.161 --> 01:15:54.602
And him and I still are very good friends today.

01:15:55.403 --> 01:16:05.546
But, you know, we were very, very different in terms of our personalities.

01:16:06.866 --> 01:16:12.848
And, you know, I really appreciated the fact that he never,

01:16:14.637 --> 01:16:25.841
He never, I mean, yeah, he pushed me too, but never did he question the things that I was doing and the life choices I was making.

01:16:26.001 --> 01:16:27.022
Right.

01:16:27.242 --> 01:16:28.742
And was always very supportive.

01:16:29.162 --> 01:16:33.824
But he was a phenomenal mentor.

01:16:33.844 --> 01:16:33.964
Wow.

01:16:34.704 --> 01:16:37.705
And probably was the reason I'm still a scientist today.

01:16:38.305 --> 01:16:39.866
Wow.

01:16:39.946 --> 01:16:40.806
How fortunate.

01:16:40.846 --> 01:16:41.727
Yeah, it is.

01:16:42.647 --> 01:16:42.887
So.

01:16:45.349 --> 01:16:55.174
Carrying on about the students and mentors, what kind of skills or values that you hope your students carry with them through their careers?

01:16:57.816 --> 01:17:05.440
So there are two things that I think are really important for all students, right?

01:17:06.100 --> 01:17:08.161
And I see it more and more now.

01:17:10.202 --> 01:17:11.243
One is that...

01:17:13.165 --> 01:17:19.787
I think that the grit probably is more important than almost anything else, right?

01:17:19.847 --> 01:17:25.529
It's the have with it-ness to get through something difficult, right?

01:17:25.789 --> 01:17:26.089
Yeah.

01:17:26.249 --> 01:17:27.429
And really push yourself.

01:17:29.110 --> 01:17:32.111
And it's far more important than getting an A.

01:17:32.551 --> 01:17:35.652
It's far more important than being able to graduate with a 4.0.

01:17:36.933 --> 01:17:42.758
It's your ability to grit out during tough times and not let it have a negative impact on you.

01:17:43.199 --> 01:17:43.899
Use it to learn.

01:17:44.019 --> 01:17:44.340
Right.

01:17:45.541 --> 01:17:47.242
I think that that's extremely important.

01:17:47.783 --> 01:17:58.012
Um, I tell all of my students all the time and my kids are so tired of hearing it that getting a degree is not a degree in wisdom.

01:17:58.392 --> 01:17:59.853
It's a degree in perseverance.

01:18:00.774 --> 01:18:01.295
And so

01:18:02.239 --> 01:18:03.600
You've got to persevere.

01:18:03.960 --> 01:18:17.029
There are times that you're going to hate it, and it's how do you get through that without hating it, without hating the process, but knowing there are going to be difficult times before you get to the end.

01:18:17.610 --> 01:18:18.890
That makes sense.

01:18:19.851 --> 01:18:24.574
So I think, yeah, that's important.

01:18:24.835 --> 01:18:28.757
And then having the education...

01:18:32.430 --> 01:19:01.628
is to open a door but it doesn't keep the door open right yes everything else about you once you get a job or once you do what you're doing um keep learning keep on uh making sure that you're becoming a better person than you are the day before right yeah um and and so i i tell people that all the time i mean

01:19:02.463 --> 01:19:03.884
The job isn't going to keep you there.

01:19:04.944 --> 01:19:08.526
At least the degree isn't going to keep you in your job, right?

01:19:08.566 --> 01:19:09.367
Right.

01:19:09.467 --> 01:19:15.690
It's going to open the door, but it's not going to be the reason you get hired, and it's not going to be the reason you keep your job.

01:19:16.130 --> 01:19:16.490
Absolutely.

01:19:16.510 --> 01:19:18.311
That's fantastic.

01:19:18.651 --> 01:19:21.913
I'm going to take that advice.

01:19:22.053 --> 01:19:29.417
So what has it meant to you personally or even professionally to be part of Texas A&M?

01:19:34.295 --> 01:19:41.058
Yeah, I mean, I'll start off by saying I'm proud to be part of Texas A&M, right?

01:19:42.259 --> 01:19:44.360
It's a phenomenal university.

01:19:45.520 --> 01:19:49.422
And you'll probably hear a lot of people talk about the core values.

01:19:50.823 --> 01:19:52.164
And I think that that's true.

01:19:53.504 --> 01:20:00.708
But I think what makes me proud is the type of people

01:20:01.906 --> 01:20:11.691
that come here as students and as faculty members and staff, for the most part, are good people, right?

01:20:11.711 --> 01:20:12.391
I agree.

01:20:12.531 --> 01:20:20.555
So, yeah, they want the best for themselves, for their students, and they're just very, very good people.

01:20:21.776 --> 01:20:28.699
Now, beyond that, what I'm proud about is that almost any time you say,

01:20:30.272 --> 01:20:59.562
you're from texas a m in some way however you that comes into discussion very seldom do you does it come with a negative connotation right so it's what the people have done before we've been here to have built something fairly strong right that has given it a reputation to have grown good people that have gone out and made a difference

01:21:00.946 --> 01:21:06.168
And so I guess that's what I think about it.

01:21:06.308 --> 01:21:06.788
I'm proud.

01:21:07.188 --> 01:21:12.230
I tell people all the time how lucky we are to be a part of Texas A&M.

01:21:12.530 --> 01:21:14.630
I feel the same.

01:21:16.731 --> 01:21:18.892
So switching over topics a little bit.

01:21:18.912 --> 01:21:19.052
Sure.

01:21:19.672 --> 01:21:26.874
How has the field of animal science and specifically reproduction processes changed since your career?

01:21:29.163 --> 01:21:30.203
Yeah.

01:21:32.204 --> 01:21:36.365
I think animal science in general has significantly changed.

01:21:36.465 --> 01:21:48.828
I mean, in terms of reproduction, I think we have so many more tools now than what we did when my career started 30 years ago.

01:21:49.589 --> 01:21:54.230
One of the things that I think is quite amazing in all of this

01:21:55.770 --> 01:22:21.837
is when you're doing the work when we're doing the research and so on you don't really notice change as much but you know i'm starting to get to a stage in my career now where i'm being asked to talk about progress and change and you know when i give those talks now i'm like

01:22:22.970 --> 01:22:45.910
oh boy look what has been happening over the last 30 years look at the change but yeah so animal production is very different when I started 30 years ago the average livestock producer could survive by not implementing technology and implementing

01:22:47.028 --> 01:23:11.882
genetic selection and implementing things like this the average livestock producer today cannot afford not to do that cannot afford to not implement modern technology right okay and so what you're seeing now is the average farmer still or rancher is still in the high 50s or low 60s

01:23:13.903 --> 01:23:22.409
But you're seeing a brand new generation of people that are coming in and entering animal science that have this expertise in technology.

01:23:23.189 --> 01:23:30.834
They're bringing it into the operations and operations are starting to adopt it at a faster and faster rate.

01:23:32.435 --> 01:23:33.796
That's so fascinating.

01:23:37.467 --> 01:23:47.012
I don't even know if this has an answer, but from an outsider, I was wondering, is there an ideal cattle industry?

01:23:47.613 --> 01:23:49.994
And if there is, what would that look like?

01:23:50.014 --> 01:23:53.416
That's a good question.

01:23:53.436 --> 01:23:56.797
I don't know if there's an ideal cattle industry.

01:23:58.458 --> 01:23:59.119
I think...

01:24:04.031 --> 01:24:24.178
I'm just thinking, you know, and the reason is that I think there's no ideal cattle industry is not only, I mean, there might be an ideal way to raise cattle today and meet certain markets that works for you today.

01:24:24.198 --> 01:24:25.559
Okay, yeah.

01:24:26.598 --> 01:24:52.770
but tomorrow the world around that industry is that that operation is different that they have to adapt that makes sense does that right okay and so i'll explain uh how i mean this take a look at how the consumer has changed in their perception of beef okay in the 80s and 90s people love beef right we ate a lot of beef

01:24:54.025 --> 01:25:21.535
and so the cattle industry then was just producing beef and it wasn't necessarily the highest quality beef people just loved it but the eating experience was variable you could go to the store and you could buy a steak and go and cook it and sometimes you'd like it and sometimes you wouldn't right yeah because you might not have prepared it well the product might not have been very good right the the industry in the u.s

01:25:22.683 --> 01:25:50.805
really changed and they started improving the quality right and so producers who might have been able to fit that market and at that time now have to adjust their genetics because the industry demanded it but then the consumer had also got into a situation where red meat is bad for you fat is bad for you so

01:25:52.285 --> 01:26:16.935
um so the cattle industry is changing and adjusting to that and now we're in a situation where thank goodness people have started actually seeing the science and it's not necessarily the red meat it's the carbohydrates it's bread and sugar and things right uh where protein animal protein actually has a lot of really good benefits right

01:26:18.228 --> 01:26:26.273
And so now we're in a market where marbling in cattle is extremely important.

01:26:26.333 --> 01:26:32.376
Marbling is the intramuscular fat that actually helps us have more tender products.

01:26:32.676 --> 01:26:33.797
Oh, like Wagyu and stuff like that.

01:26:33.817 --> 01:26:34.838
Yeah, exactly.

01:26:34.878 --> 01:26:38.440
Wagyu and then most Angus genetics products.

01:26:40.501 --> 01:26:45.584
And so when people cook that meat, it's more difficult to screw it up.

01:26:46.024 --> 01:26:46.665
I see.

01:26:47.840 --> 01:27:12.812
so they have a better eating experience right and and now we also have um people that make more money and are willing to pay more for their for better quality beef right so the producers that are not have not started producing animals that have better genetics the cattle markets have now shifted that were in the 80s and 90s

01:27:15.081 --> 01:27:25.786
It didn't matter what size animal went through the sale barn to slaughter, every animal was worth the same price based on weight.

01:27:26.346 --> 01:27:27.526
That's not the case anymore.

01:27:28.387 --> 01:27:34.329
An animal that was really good at marbling genetics is worth about $1,000 more than an animal that isn't, right?

01:27:35.109 --> 01:28:03.987
so can you imagine being a producer that was producing poor quality beef that in the 80s and 90s might have been doing a good job so they didn't adjust so it's really difficult to pinpoint what the ideal operation is and every country has a different system that makes sense but at the same time I think the ideal operation is the operation that is willing to adjust to what the consumer wants

01:28:08.205 --> 01:28:08.565
Perfect.

01:28:09.926 --> 01:28:13.589
I didn't think that was ever going to have an answer because it's too complicated.

01:28:15.050 --> 01:28:16.431
But I think you put that very well.

01:28:17.192 --> 01:28:18.913
Yeah.

01:28:20.534 --> 01:28:31.102
Are there any, I know we talked about technology and changes in the way you've approached things, but is there something that really excites you now that you see coming into the future?

01:28:31.623 --> 01:28:33.404
Any technologies or process?

01:28:33.424 --> 01:28:33.684
Yeah.

01:28:36.320 --> 01:28:49.825
Yeah, I think we touched on this a little bit, but I do think what really excites me right now is I think we're starting to see that people value where their food comes from.

01:28:51.291 --> 01:29:02.718
And then coupled with that is the exponential increase in our ability to use data to make decisions and decision aid tools and tools.

01:29:03.419 --> 01:29:13.085
So AI, machine learning, sensor technology, and then a consumer starting to value where the food comes from.

01:29:13.945 --> 01:29:14.646
I think those two.

01:29:17.820 --> 01:29:23.185
And these last couple questions are like personal reflections kind of questions.

01:29:25.487 --> 01:29:34.054
Has there been any turning points in your career, whether that's good or bad, that significantly changed the trajectory of your life or career in general?

01:29:35.375 --> 01:29:40.339
Yeah, so, yeah, multiple, right?

01:29:40.379 --> 01:29:40.820
I think...

01:29:42.122 --> 01:29:46.003
And this might be something, you know, back to the mentoring.

01:29:46.263 --> 01:29:52.946
One of the things I tell my students all the time when they're trying to make decisions is, is it the right decision?

01:29:53.566 --> 01:29:55.106
It's not really the right decision.

01:29:55.526 --> 01:29:59.848
When you think it's Yogi Berra said, when you come to a fork in the road, take it.

01:30:00.208 --> 01:30:01.048
Right?

01:30:01.548 --> 01:30:09.191
So you're going to come to multiple times in your life where you have decisions to make.

01:30:10.334 --> 01:30:12.339
Don't sit there and pine over the decision.

01:30:12.399 --> 01:30:14.103
Make the decision and don't look back.

01:30:14.224 --> 01:30:15.026
Right.

01:30:15.266 --> 01:30:19.637
So in my life, I mean, I think the decision.

01:30:20.961 --> 01:30:50.084
at a very young age not knowing anybody in the u.s and coming to study as a teenager to leave a family in africa right right um and not knowing what the future held but that my plan was always to go back so that was one i think um not necessarily having uh the opportunity to go back to africa was a big

01:30:51.070 --> 01:30:54.912
turning point in my career and don't regret it at all.

01:30:55.772 --> 01:31:05.997
I'm just sad when it happened but I think I've been able to have a larger impact even on my home country doing what I'm doing than had I gone back.

01:31:06.197 --> 01:31:07.278
That's got to feel good.

01:31:07.678 --> 01:31:08.398
Yeah, that does.

01:31:10.399 --> 01:31:15.002
And then probably the last really big decision was

01:31:16.369 --> 01:31:37.600
i never really thought of being a university administrator right um never crossed my mind it was never something that i aspired to do but taking the leap when somebody asked me if i would consider it i think has been has been life-changing and

01:31:39.567 --> 01:31:49.776
I look back at it now and every administrative position I've had, I've been able to have a positive impact on more and more people.

01:31:50.177 --> 01:31:54.460
And so those are probably the big ones.

01:31:54.480 --> 01:31:55.041
Absolutely.

01:32:01.007 --> 01:32:12.712
You've already given some advice, but is there any other kind of advice that you would like to give to people who maybe are interested in this field or just interested in this field?

01:32:17.094 --> 01:32:21.376
Yeah, I think there is some advice, right?

01:32:21.496 --> 01:32:28.679
I think the folks that will make the biggest impact in the future

01:32:30.766 --> 01:32:43.436
In my field, and I'll just talk about animal science in general, and maybe even over the entire AgriLife research, right?

01:32:44.838 --> 01:32:55.966
The thing that I would say are those that are going to be the most successful are going to be people who are willing to adapt faster.

01:32:56.847 --> 01:32:57.968
And I'll give you an example.

01:32:59.478 --> 01:33:19.002
in the past if we were going to look for a faculty member or look for somebody to lead a certain area we would look we would say well we need somebody who's a geneticist right and then we go out and look for somebody who fits a position description

01:33:20.830 --> 01:33:34.114
But now when we do that, yeah, we may broadly want a geneticist, but really what we want is we want somebody who over the next 30 to 40 years is going to be able to adapt to needs over that period of time.

01:33:34.555 --> 01:33:37.575
Because if we hire a geneticist today,

01:33:39.968 --> 01:33:59.044
30 years from now genetics may not be the focus that we need right we need somebody who's willing to adapt as time changes so i think adaptability is extremely important for anybody for young people i would say

01:34:00.865 --> 01:34:27.991
most of them want to come out and make a lot of money right off the bat almost everybody wants to do that and i would let everybody know to be patient right make sure that you spend your first five or ten years out of school honing your skills you may not it may not be the most the greatest income generator you may not become a millionaire in your first five or ten years but build

01:34:28.827 --> 01:34:37.181
on that and then gradually grow through the system and and and take on opportunities yeah don't rush it yeah

01:34:38.721 --> 01:34:47.248
I think we have too many people that want to leave and go and start a job right away because they see the dollar signs, but they don't see the investments in themselves.

01:34:47.308 --> 01:34:47.548
Right.

01:34:48.329 --> 01:34:49.990
That's fantastic.

01:34:50.330 --> 01:34:55.435
I have two legacy questions from, they've been asked for the last 50 years in these oral histories.

01:34:55.935 --> 01:35:05.943
The first is, if you were given a million dollars and you had to use it somehow on this university, does it matter where or when, just your pick, what would you put that to?

01:35:07.024 --> 01:35:27.438
If I had a million dollars, I would probably put it into an endowment so that it would live forever and use the interest from that to focus on graduate education.

01:35:28.124 --> 01:35:28.864
Fantastic.

01:35:28.884 --> 01:35:31.866
First person who says that to me, by the way.

01:35:31.906 --> 01:35:33.427
First person who's told me that.

01:35:33.567 --> 01:35:34.267
Oh, really?

01:35:34.287 --> 01:35:35.048
Yeah, that's, yeah.

01:35:35.068 --> 01:35:36.769
It's a unique answer.

01:35:38.269 --> 01:35:47.034
And my very last question is, how do you want to be remembered in your career or personal life?

01:35:47.114 --> 01:35:48.435
That's a good question.

01:35:50.089 --> 01:35:51.030
I don't know.

01:35:51.090 --> 01:35:54.651
I haven't really thought about how I want to be remembered.

01:35:55.572 --> 01:36:10.840
My hope is that when I hang up my shoes and I'm six feet under, that people will remember me for having an impact on other people's lives.

01:36:10.980 --> 01:36:17.744
I think the accolades in terms of science and things like that, I think those are great, but I think that's not really what drives me.

01:36:22.489 --> 01:36:29.473
And I really want them to remember that I was hugely passionate about agriculture.

01:36:30.414 --> 01:36:31.154
Absolutely.

01:36:32.915 --> 01:36:37.238
This is the first time I've physically met you in the flesh, and I can even feel that.

01:36:37.318 --> 01:36:40.019
So I'm sure that's going to be it.

01:36:40.039 --> 01:36:41.460
It's going to be your legacy.

01:36:41.480 --> 01:36:42.601
Yeah.

01:36:43.481 --> 01:36:46.263
Is there any questions or comments that you wanted to...

01:36:47.978 --> 01:36:49.179
Wanted to talk about that?

01:36:49.339 --> 01:36:50.239
Yeah, I think so.

01:36:51.240 --> 01:36:52.801
Thank you so much for doing this, by the way.

01:36:52.821 --> 01:36:54.221
Oh, anytime.

01:36:54.241 --> 01:36:55.262
If you need more, let me know.

01:36:55.402 --> 01:36:55.922
Absolutely.

